Comment: This dataset aggregates entries from many upstream sources, each
 carrying its own terms; see docs/SOURCE_LICENSES.md for the complete
 per-source enumeration. Two verbatim text bodies embedded in data/incidents.json
 by scripts/ingest_external.py are NOT covered by this CC-BY-4.0 grant and
 remain Apache-2.0: MITRE ATLAS case-study text (Copyright 2021-2026 The
 MITRE Corporation) and garak probe-docstring text (Copyright 2023 Leon
 Derczynski and NVIDIA). AIAAIC-derived entries carry no BY-SA subset in
 this CC-BY-4.0 grant per decision D2 (title/headline + short
 non-narrative facts -- system, technology, sector, jurisdiction, and
 the affected developer/deployer, an organisation name and not a
 taxonomy tag -- plus a source pointer kept, no verbatim AIAAIC
 narrative prose) -- but AIAAIC's own CC BY-SA 4.0 grant and a parallel
 EU/UK sui generis database-right question remain an OPEN, unresolved
 item, pending AIAAIC's reply (outreach sent 2026-07-27) or a
 qualified-counsel resolution.
 genai_incidents itself holds no UK/EU database right (sole
 Canada-resident individual maker), so the worst case is row-level
 attribution/share-alike on AIAAIC-derived rows, never dataset-wide --
 honored per-row via the content_license marker on every AIAAIC-citing
 entry (both the description_source == "aiaaic" rows and the
 hand-curated rows marked via title/categorical-facts descent -- current
 count audited in docs/SOURCE_LICENSES.md section 1.1), which also
 covers the retained headline itself. See NOTICE-DATA and
 docs/SOURCE_LICENSES.md section 1.1 for the full framing. AIID-derived
 entries (ingested via AIID's official snapshot, SOURCE_LICENSES.md
 section 1.2a) likewise carry no BY-SA subset -- only the AIID-authored
 title and structured facts are kept, never the licensed narrative.
 AIID's content-licensing question is RESOLVED, not open, for the
 ~1,463-row population that ships (0 of 1,466 rows carry a
 content_license marker under every measurable definition -- tag,
 aiid_id field, AIID-<n> source-ID, or reference URL). AIID's maker is
 US-situated, so U.S. Circular 33 excludes the retained title from
 copyright, and that same US situs means AIID's maker fails both the
 UK and EU sui generis database-right qualification tests, so no such
 right subsists in AIID's collections -- the opposite posture from
 AIAAIC's still-open UK-situated question, not the same one. This
 does not cover the 63 hand-curated rows in
 ingest/aiid_incidents.json or the 1,457 AIRI Navigator rows sharing
 AIID's source-ID convention, which stay out of the shipped corpus
 only by merge-order mechanics and reopen for review if that changes.
 See NOTICE-DATA and docs/SOURCE_LICENSES.md section 1.2a for the full
 framing, and docs/audits/E23-aiid-marking-ruling-2026-07-30.md for
 the full reasoning.
 The OECD AI Incidents and Hazards Monitor (AIM) is the last of the
 ten sources NOTICE-DATA names as carrying an obligation. With AIAAIC and AIID, it is one of the three
 whose row text this project could not simply carry under the source's
 own licence grant. For each of the three, the text was either reduced
 to structural facts and a link, or its retained title was analysed as
 a separate copyright question (open for AIAAIC and AIM; resolved for
 AIID). For six of the other seven named there (MITRE ATLAS, garak, the
 CVE Program, AVID, the OWASP Top 10 for Agentic Applications and the
 OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications), the grant (for the two OWASP
 mapping files, the carve-out stanzas below) covers the material reproduced, and the obligation is attribution or a notice that accompanies copies
 (CVE- and AVID-derived rows also carry a row-level content_license
 marker). The seventh, the OWASP Gen AI incident round-ups, is an
 exception: two shipped entries (INC-02646, INC-03233) carried CC BY-SA 4.0
 round-up text up to and including v2.13.0 and were rewritten from their
 primary sources on 2026-10-13 (decision D97); see NOTICE-DATA and docs/SOURCE_LICENSES.md section 1.6.
 .
 AIM's title/summary fields are LLM-generated (OpenAI
 o3-mini) from third-party news articles of unresolved copyright
 status; AIM's own methodology page disclaims any grant over such
 third-party-protected material, and OECD's general Data-reuse terms
 permit extraction and redistribution of OECD's own IP with
 attribution but do not cover that narrative text -- OECD's terms are
 not presented here as a grant over it. Per decision E21 (2026-07-30),
 the description field is reduced to structural facts and a source
 link only -- never summary/evidences -- and per-entry OECD
 attribution (OECD (year), AI Incidents and Hazards Monitor, url
 (accessed on date)) is applied to every OECD-sourced row; both are
 implemented and merged. The title field remains OPEN, not resolved
 and not silent: it still ships the same LLM-generated text verbatim
 on 3,937 of 4,104 non-retracted OECD-AIM-sourced rows (measured
 2026-10-12 by direct comparison of each title with its ingest title in
 ingest/oecd_aim_full_incidents.json; see NOTICE-DATA and
 docs/audits/v2.13.0-cut-package-2026-10-12.md section 4R row C3) -- the
 other 167 were merged into an entry whose own text, from a different
 upstream source, took precedence instead -- tracked at the same posture as AIAAIC's
 retained-headline question above. See NOTICE-DATA and
 docs/SOURCE_LICENSES.md section 1.5 for the full framing.
